"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,351\nEpisode 3\n\n3\n00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:09,577\nDon\u2019t read your slides\n\n4\n00:00:12,377 --> 00:00:13,719\nA slide full of text,\n\n5\n00:00:13,739 --> 00:00:15,921\nand an instructor reading it aloud,\n\n6\n00:00:15,941 --> 00:00:16,989\nword for word.\n\n7\n00:00:17,415 --> 00:00:20,083\nStudents hear it and read it at the same time.\n\n8\n00:00:20,316 --> 00:00:21,373\nTwice the input.\n\n9\n00:00:21,689 --> 00:00:22,838\nTwice the learning?\n\n10\n00:00:23,008 --> 00:00:31,599\nIn Mayer's research, adding on-screen text that duplicates the narration to an animation actually hurt learning on transfer tests.\n\n11\n00:00:32,068 --> 00:00:37,334\nThe redundancy principle: the median effect size was zero point eight six,\n\n12\n00:00:37,592 --> 00:00:40,428\npositive in sixteen of sixteen tests.\n\n13\n00:00:40,598 --> 00:00:43,421\nA related finding is the modality principle.\n\n14\n00:00:43,837 --> 00:00:49,241\nStudents learned better from graphics with spoken words than from graphics with printed words.\n\n15\n00:00:49,637 --> 00:00:52,258\nIn Mayer's 2021 summary,\n\n16\n00:00:52,517 --> 00:00:55,216\nthe median effect was one point zero,\n\n17\n00:00:55,527 --> 00:00:58,381\npositive in eighteen of nineteen tests.\n\n18\n00:00:58,551 --> 00:00:59,104\nWhy?\n\n19\n00:00:59,286 --> 00:01:02,785\nReading and listening to the same words compete for attention.\n\n20\n00:01:03,288 --> 00:01:05,302\nAnd when the eyes are busy reading,\n\n21\n00:01:05,608 --> 00:01:08,622\nthey are not looking at the graphic that the words describe.\n\n22\n00:01:08,792 --> 00:01:10,179\nAn important caveat.\n\n23\n00:01:10,613 --> 00:01:14,319\nThese studies tested verbatim text on screen for everyone.\n\n24\n00:01:14,792 --> 00:01:16,579\nOptional captions are different.\n\n25\n00:01:17,034 --> 00:01:20,560\nThey support students with hearing loss and others who need them,\n\n26\n00:01:20,776 --> 00:01:24,507\nand some research suggests they can help non native speakers.\n\n27\n00:01:24,887 --> 00:01:26,500\nKeep captions available.\n\n28\n00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:30,178\nMost of this research used short narrated animations,\n\n29\n00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,751\nnot live lectures.\n\n30\n00:01:32,116 --> 00:01:35,744\nApplying it to a live class is a reasonable extension,\n\n31\n00:01:36,019 --> 00:01:37,107\nbut an extension.\n\n32\n00:01:37,277 --> 00:01:38,042\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:38,394 --> 00:01:42,526\nPut a few key words and a strong visual on each slide,\n\n34\n00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:44,657\nand say the rest.\n\n35\n00:01:45,064 --> 00:01:48,137\nDon't read your slides aloud.\n\n36\n00:01:48,348 --> 00:01:50,819\nMove detailed text into a handout or notes.\n\n37\n00:01:51,288 --> 00:01:53,161\nAnd keep captions on for recorded video.\n\n38\n00:01:53,415 --> 00:01:56,099\nOne tip to share with students: turn dense slides into your own words.\n\n39\n00:01:56,269 --> 00:01:58,177\nThe slide supports the speaker.\n\n40\n00:01:58,355 --> 00:01:59,660\nIt shouldn't be the script.\n\n"